# Nouns Reference Content

> The per-paradigm tables under "Noun Paradigms by Gender" are reference data for
> `AGREEMENT_PARADIGMS`, not the page layout: the page renders them as case × pattern
> grids (see "Page Structure"). Where this file disagrees with `components/nouns-section.tsx`,
> the code is the truth.

Noun declensions by gender and case - how Greek nouns change their endings.

## Purpose

Show how Greek nouns decline according to gender, case, and number. The ending patterns reveal the underlying system.

## Key Insight

The ending gives away the gender, and each gender makes one move in the singular: masculine drops its -ς for the Target, feminine adds a -ς for the Owner, neuter keeps its Doer form for the Target. Most of a noun table is the dictionary form left alone. `src/constants/agreement.test.ts` holds every paradigm to these rules and to the plural and vocative notes below.

---

## Page Structure

<page file="tabs/nouns.tsx">
1. ReferenceHero "Masculine drops a ς. Feminine gains one." Thesis: the ending tells you the gender, the gender tells you where the ending changes. No demo.
2. Band 1 (sr-only h2 "What each gender changes"): three gender TeachingCards (`GENDER_MOVES` in `components/nouns-section.tsx`), then a ruled notes list (`NOUN_NOTES`). See "What Each Gender Changes".
3. Band 2 "Look it up": the four core patterns (masc-os, fem-a, neut-o, neut-i) in one `GrammarTable` inside a bordered Card. Case rows (Doer / Target / Owner), one column per pattern headed by its word (φίλος, γυναίκα, βιβλίο, παιδί), a gender chip over each run of same-gender columns (one "neuter" spans βιβλίο and παιδί), Singular and Plural as row groups under one header.
4. "Every pattern in the course" (the only collapsible): per gender, a grid of that gender's remaining patterns (no chips; the heading names the gender), then a ruled list of every pattern of that gender with up to three corpus words, the corpus count ("N nouns in the course") and the paradigm's tip. An example word must end in the pattern's ending and decline the way the pattern does: a noun whose stored forms the pattern does not generate (μπαμπάς, filed under -ας for its singular but plural μπαμπάδες) is counted but never shown.
5. Band 3 "Nouns sorted. Now describe them.": NextStepCards to Adjectives (emphasis), Articles, and /learn/nouns.
6. PracticeCTA "Practice nouns".

Cells render article, stem and ending, with three weights on the ending: the Doer is medium, an ending that differs from the Doer is semibold, one identical to it is muted. Below `sm` a cell shows the ending alone and the column header supplies the word; columns size to content there and are fixed-width from `sm` up.
</page>

---

## What Each Gender Changes

<teaching-cards source="GENDER_MOVES in components/nouns-section.tsx">
One TeachingCard per gender, tinted in that gender's scheme. Each row is a paradigm: its ending, the Doer, an arrow, and the form the rule is about. Rows are derived from `AGREEMENT_PARADIGMS`, and the bold falls where the two forms part company (stress-blind): on the letters the new form gains, or when it only loses some, on the letters the Doer gives up.

| Card      | Rule                                                        | Rows (paradigm ids)                  | Shows           |
|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------|
| Masculine | Drops the -ς when it's the Target.                          | masc-os, masc-as, masc-is, masc-es   | ο φίλο**ς** → τον φίλο |
| Feminine  | Adds a -ς when it's the Owner.                              | fem-a, fem-i, fem-si                 | η ζωή → της ζωή**ς** |
| Neuter    | Doer and Target are the same word. Only the Owner changes.  | neut-o, neut-i, neut-ma              | το όνομα → του ονόμα**τος** |

</teaching-cards>

<notes source="NOUN_NOTES">
Greek in the notes stays neutral with weight on the morpheme: the cards carry gender colour, and case colour beside them would put both axes in one view.
- A few neuters dress as masculines: το μέρος, το κρέας. When ending and article disagree, trust the article.
- In the plural, Doer and Target match, except masculines in -ος: οι φίλοι but τους φίλους.
- Every plural Owner ends in -ων: των φίλων, των γυναικών, των παιδιών.
- Calling someone uses the Target form without its article (Γιάννη!, πατέρα!); -ος nouns usually switch to -ε (φίλε!).
</notes>

---

## Noun Paradigms by Gender

### Masculine Patterns

<paradigm name="masculine-os" frequency="very common">
<pattern>-ος</pattern>
<example>φίλος (friend)</example>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | ο φίλος  | οι φίλοι |
| Acc  | τον φίλο | τους φίλους |
| Gen  | του φίλου | των φίλων |
| Voc  | φίλε! | φίλοι! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="masculine-as" frequency="common">
<pattern>-ας</pattern>
<example>πατέρας (father)</example>
<tip>When the -άς itself is stressed, the plural adds a syllable instead: μπαμπάς → μπαμπάδες</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | ο πατέρας | οι πατέρες |
| Acc  | τον πατέρα | τους πατέρες |
| Gen  | του πατέρα | των πατέρων |
| Voc  | πατέρα! | πατέρες! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="masculine-is" frequency="common">
<pattern>-ης</pattern>
<example>μαθητής (student)</example>
<tip>Words in -τζής add a syllable in the plural instead: ταξιτζής → ταξιτζήδες</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | ο μαθητής | οι μαθητές |
| Acc  | τον μαθητή | τους μαθητές |
| Gen  | του μαθητή | των μαθητών |
| Voc  | μαθητή! | μαθητές! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="masculine-es" frequency="less common">
<pattern>-ές</pattern>
<example>καφές (coffee)</example>
<tip>Often borrowed words: καφές, καναπές</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | ο καφές | οι καφέδες |
| Acc  | τον καφέ | τους καφέδες |
| Gen  | του καφέ | των καφέδων |
| Voc  | καφέ! | καφέδες! |

</paradigm>

### Feminine Patterns

<paradigm name="feminine-a" frequency="very common">
<pattern>-α</pattern>
<example>γυναίκα (woman/wife)</example>
| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | η γυναίκα | οι γυναίκες |
| Acc  | τη γυναίκα | τις γυναίκες |
| Gen  | της γυναίκας | των γυναικών |
| Voc  | γυναίκα! | γυναίκες! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="feminine-i" frequency="very common">
<pattern>-η</pattern>
<example>ζωή (life)</example>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | η ζωή | οι ζωές |
| Acc  | τη ζωή | τις ζωές |
| Gen  | της ζωής | των ζωών |
| Voc  | ζωή! | ζωές! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="feminine-si" frequency="common">
<pattern>-ση</pattern>
<example>ερώτηση (question)</example>
<note>-ξη (απόδειξη) and -ψη (άποψη) are separate paradigms with the same endings — the consonant is kept: αποδείξεις, απόψεις</note>
<tip>Action nouns (like English -tion)</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | η ερώτηση | οι ερωτήσεις |
| Acc  | την ερώτηση | τις ερωτήσεις |
| Gen  | της ερώτησης | των ερωτήσεων |
| Voc  | ερώτηση! | ερωτήσεις! |

</paradigm>

### Neuter Patterns

<paradigm name="neuter-o" frequency="very common">
<pattern>-ο</pattern>
<example>βιβλίο (book)</example>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | το βιβλίο | τα βιβλία |
| Acc  | το βιβλίο | τα βιβλία |
| Gen  | του βιβλίου | των βιβλίων |
| Voc  | βιβλίο! | βιβλία! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="neuter-i" frequency="common">
<pattern>-ι</pattern>
<example>παιδί (child)</example>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | το παιδί | τα παιδιά |
| Acc  | το παιδί | τα παιδιά |
| Gen  | του παιδιού | των παιδιών |
| Voc  | παιδί! | παιδιά! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="neuter-ma" frequency="common">
<pattern>-μα</pattern>
<example>όνομα (name)</example>
<tip>Adds -τ- everywhere but the singular Doer and Target: ονόματος, ονόματα</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | το όνομα | τα ονόματα |
| Acc  | το όνομα | τα ονόματα |
| Gen  | του ονόματος | των ονομάτων |
| Voc  | όνομα! | ονόματα! |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="neuter-os" frequency="less common">
<pattern>-ος</pattern>
<example>μέρος (part / place)</example>
<tip>Looks masculine but is neuter: το μέρος, τα μέρη</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | το μέρος | τα μέρη |
| Acc  | το μέρος | τα μέρη |
| Gen  | του μέρους | των μερών |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="neuter-as" frequency="less common">
<pattern>-ας</pattern>
<example>κρέας (meat)</example>
<tip>Adds -τ- before the ending, like -μα words</tip>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | το κρέας | τα κρέατα |
| Acc  | το κρέας | τα κρέατα |
| Gen  | του κρέατος | των κρεάτων |

</paradigm>

<paradigm name="feminine-i-archaic" frequency="common">
<pattern>-η (πόλη)</pattern>
<example>πόλη (city)</example>
<note>Cannot be inferred from the ending: πόλη takes -εις but αγάπη takes -ες.
Membership is a hand-kept list in `DECLENSION_BY_LEMMA`
(`src/scripts/seed-data/vocabulary/noun-seed-enrichment.ts`).</note>

| Case | Singular | Plural |
|------|----------|--------|
| Nom  | η πόλη | οι πόλεις |
| Acc  | την πόλη | τις πόλεις |
| Gen  | της πόλης | των πόλεων |

</paradigm>

---

## Vocative

Not given a section or a table row. The curriculum guidance is that the vocative earns
incidental exposure, not drilling, so it is the last of the notes under the gender cards:
the Target form without its article, except -ος nouns, which usually end in -ε. The rule
holds for all 15 paradigms (`agreement.test.ts`); masc-os is the one exception.

